  "title": "Hoy Como en Casa --- The Pieces That Move",
  "drivablePrefix": 2,
  "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:cce63f202f721fc2",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "ES-C67-hoy-como-en-casa",
      "sequence": 2560,
      "title": "hoy como en casa — the pieces that move",
      "headword": "hoy como en casa",
      "romanization": "hoy como en casa",
      "gloss": "today I eat at home — the parts that say when and where can move, and what that freedom is actually for",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:a55f0bc0c69c93c9",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Como en casa. — I eat at home. Now add hoy, and put it wherever sounds right to you."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Whatever you chose was probably correct, and that is the lesson."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: three placements, one meaning",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Hoy como en casa. Como hoy en casa. Como en casa hoy."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "All three are Spanish, and all three mean the same thing. The pieces that say when and where are not pinned to a position the way the subject and the verb are."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You can stack them, too: Hoy como en casa con María. When, where, with whom — and the order among them is yours."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The freedom is not free, and it is not decoration. The front of the sentence is the emphatic position. Putting hoy first says today, as against some other day. Leaving it at the end mentions it and moves on."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So the choice is not stylistic. It is how a Spanish speaker does what an English speaker does by stressing a word out loud."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And notice why this is possible at all. The verb ending already says who is acting, and the personal a already marks who is on the receiving end — so nothing depends on position to keep the roles straight. Latin's endings paid for word order when they fell; the pieces Spanish kept are what let the rest move."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "all three placements of hoy",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: all three placements of *hoy*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "Hoy como en casa as though correcting somebody about the day",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *Hoy como en casa* as though correcting somebody about the day]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "repeat",
              "times": 2,
              "source": "[REPEAT x2]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C67-hoy-como-en-casa-three",
              "prompt": "How many places can hoy go in Como en casa?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-CIRCUMSTANTIAL-MOVES"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 13,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "three",
                "three: front, middle, end"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "All three are correct Spanish.",
                "incorrect": "Three -- front, middle or end."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C67-hoy-como-en-casa-front",
              "prompt": "What does putting hoy at the front do?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-WORD-ORDER-EMPHASIS"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 14,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "emphasises it",
                "stresses today as against another day"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "The front is the emphatic position.",
                "incorrect": "It emphasises it -- today, not some other day."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C67-hoy-como-en-casa-why",
              "prompt": "Why can Spanish move these pieces safely?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-CIRCUMSTANTIAL-MOVES"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 15,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "the endings already say who does what",
                "because agreement and the personal a keep the roles clear"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Nothing depends on position for the roles.",
                "incorrect": "Because the endings already carry who is doing what."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which position is the emphatic one? (The front.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And what makes the freedom safe? (The endings already say who is doing what.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ES-C67-repaso-la-terminacion-y-el-infinitivo",
      "sequence": 2561,
      "title": "Review — what the ending carries, and what happens when it carries nothing",
      "headword": "(review)",
      "romanization": "(review)",
      "gloss": "the ending carries the person, which is why the pronoun can go — and the two forms that carry no person are exactly the two that stop behaving like verbs",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:c1afec1769fee7b6",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "\"We go.\" (Vamos.) \"They go.\" (Van.) No pronoun needed for either."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The ending says who. That one fact has been paying for two things at once."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It pays for the missing pronoun. Van is three letters and it still tells you the subject is plural and is not you. Nothing was lost when ellos went; the information was never in ellos."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And it pays for a second reading. Hablo is \"I speak\" and also \"I am speaking.\" The progressive was never required — estoy hablando is available, not obligatory."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So vamos, vais, van each do the same double duty: a person, and a right-now."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Now turn it around. What happens to a verb form that names no person at all?"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It stops being a verb and starts behaving like a noun."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Comer es bueno. — Eating is good."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Comer has no ending saying who, so nothing anchors it to a subject, and it can sit in the subject slot itself. English needs two forms here and argues about which; Spanish sends the infinitive and has never needed the argument."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And one step further along the same road:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "comer becomes la comida."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Take the ending off and put -ida on, and the verb becomes a noun outright — with a gender, an article, and a plural. The further a form gets from saying who, the more like a noun it behaves."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "hablo, hablas, habla — and drop every pronoun as you go",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *hablo, hablas, habla* — and drop every pronoun as you go]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "comer, la comida — a verb, then the same verb as a thing",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *comer*, *la comida* — a verb, then the same verb as a thing]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "repeat",
              "times": 2,
              "source": "[REPEAT x2]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C67-repaso-la-terminacion-y-el-infinitivo-prodrop",
              "prompt": "Say you all go, with no pronoun. Then say what tells the listener who.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-AGREEMENT-EXPLAINS-PRO-DROP",
                "ES-GRAMMAR-IR-VERB-PRESENT-2PL"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 12,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "vais; the ending",
                "vais, the ending does",
                "vais - the ending"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "The -ais carries it, so vosotros can stay unsaid.",
                "incorrect": "Vais — and the ending is what says who."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C67-repaso-la-terminacion-y-el-infinitivo-dos",
              "prompt": "Give the two English readings of hablo.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-PRESENT-ACTUAL-AND-DURATIVE",
                "ES-GRAMMAR-PROGRESSIVE-IS-OPTIONAL"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 12,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "i speak, and i am speaking",
                "i speak / i am speaking"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Both, always — the progressive is an option, not a requirement.",
                "incorrect": "I speak, and I am speaking."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C67-repaso-la-terminacion-y-el-infinitivo-sujeto",
              "prompt": "Say eating is good.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-INFINITIVE-AS-SUBJECT",
                "ES-GRAMMAR-INFINITIVE-NOT-GERUND"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 10,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "comer es bueno",
                "comer es bueno."
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "The infinitive, not a gerund.",
                "incorrect": "Comer es bueno."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C67-repaso-la-terminacion-y-el-infinitivo-nombre",
              "prompt": "Turn comer into a noun with an article.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-NOUN-FROM-VERB-IDA",
                "ES-LEX-COMIDA"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 10,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "la comida",
                "comida"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "A gender and an article — a noun outright.",
                "incorrect": "La comida."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Why can Spanish drop the subject pronoun? (The ending says who.) And what can the infinitive do that a conjugated form cannot? (Stand where a noun stands — comer es bueno.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
